  "default_backend_statement": "The object backend stays the package default. Phase 2M-A's committed record prices it and Phase 2M-D's equivalence matrix has to rebuild it, so a phase that changed the default before its own gate was graded would invalidate the baseline it is graded against.",
  "default_storage_backend": "object",
  "equivalence_gate": {
    "required": true,
    "statement": "Every priced bank is built under both backends and compared before any byte is reported: S and H bitwise equal via numpy array_equal, identical word universe, identical T_coeff, identical basis labels. A storage change that moved a matrix element would not be a storage change, and a byte reduction quoted from a bank whose answers drifted is worthless. The producer refuses to emit a row that fails this.",
    "why_bitwise": "MV.trace_pairing iterates the smaller operand's dict in insertion order, and 54--84% of resident rows are smaller than the reference, so the row drives the summation for most entries. Storing a row's words ascending -- the natural CSR choice -- moves 4--17% of S/H entries by up to 3.1e-13. MatrixElementBank._build_pair promises those entries reproduce solver.projected_matrices to the last bit, so tolerance is not the standard here; equality is."
  },
  "estimand": {
    "name": "2M-B_retained_storage_reduction_of_the_packed_row_representation",
    "question": "At unchanged T_coeff and bitwise-unchanged matrix elements, how many bytes does the packed CSR/SoA representation retain against the dict-of-complex one, and on which banks does that reduction reach the 3x Phase 2M's go/no-go asks for?",
    "why_it_exists": "2M-A measured the baseline and found 85.24--96.19 bytes per retained coefficient against a packed model of 24. That established headroom, not a reduction. This record measures the reduction an implementation actually delivers, and separates the part that is a property of the row representation from the part that depends on the bank."
  },
